```

The code reads 12 bytes into `buffer[64]`. If the file contains more than 12 bytes at this point, the `ReadOK` function will write past the end of the `buffer` array, leading to a buffer overflow. This vulnerability can be exploited by providing a malicious BMP file that triggers this overflow, potentially allowing an attacker to overwrite memory and gain control of the program.

Similar vulnerabilities exist in other `ReadOK` calls within the function. The lack of size validation before reading into fixed-size buffers makes the function susceptible to buffer overflows.

