The function `GmfOpenMesh` is vulnerable to a buffer overflow in the `strcpy` call.

```c
   // Copy the FilNam into the structure
   if(strlen(FilNam) + 7 >= GmfStrSiz)
      longjmp(msh->err, -4);

   strcpy(msh->FilNam, FilNam);
```

The code checks if `strlen(FilNam) + 7` is greater than or equal to `GmfStrSiz` before calling `strcpy`. However, if the condition is true, it only calls `longjmp` which terminates the function and returns 0. It does *not* prevent the `strcpy` from being called. If `strlen(FilNam) + 7` is greater than `GmfStrSiz`, `strcpy` will write past the end of the `msh->FilNam` buffer, leading to a buffer overflow.

The `GmfStrSiz` is likely a constant defining the size of the `str` array and `msh->FilNam` member.  If the input `FilNam` is long enough, `strcpy` will overwrite memory beyond the bounds of `msh->FilNam`, potentially corrupting other data or even allowing an attacker to execute arbitrary code.
